<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bandua</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bandua was a god of the pre-Roman peoples of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, worshipped in what is now northern and central Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He is known from dozens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> altar inscriptions of the Roman period, where his name often carries an epithet linking him to a particular community. He is usually seen as a protector of communities and warriors among the Callaeci and Lusitanians.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bandua.jpg" alt="Bandua" width="320" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lanobriga · CC BY-SA 2.5 es · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ara_de_Eiras.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The name is recorded in several forms, among them Bandi, Bandei, Bandu, Bandue, Banduae and Bandiae, generally in the native Galaico-Lusitanian inflection. Older scholarship wrote it as Bandua, mistakenly taking the forms for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> ones, and Bandue is now preferred. By 2025, 47 inscriptions had been counted, including the variant spellings. According to Jürgen Untermann and Blanca María Prósper, Bandue and Bandua prevail in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a>, while Bandi or Bande is more common in the Lusitanian area to the south.</p><p>Most inscriptions give the name with an epithet, such as Bandue Veigebreago at Xinzo de Limia, Bandue Bolecco at Palas de Rei, Bandi Roudaecus at Trujillo and Bandi Haracui at Arronches, while ten carry Bandue Vorteaeco or its variants. An altar at Rairiz de Veiga names Bandua as a god of the Vexillum and partner of Mars. Six inscriptions come from the province of Ourense and others from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caceres">Cáceres</a>. With Cosus, Nabia and Reo, Bandua is one of the best documented deities of western and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, and it has been proposed that the cult spread from Callaecia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturia">Asturia</a> south into Lusitania.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Scholars disagree whether the name belonged to one deity or was an epithet given to different gods. Many epithets contain the element -briga or -bris, fortress, and seem to name settlements. Olivares Pedreño, who treats Bandua as male, linked them to ancient vici and castella far from romanised centres, and later saw the god as a protector of local native communities. According to Daniela Ferreira, most studies regard Bandua as a tutelary deity, deriving the name from the Proto-Indo-European root *bhendh-, to bind.</p><p>De Bernardo Stempel reads the epithets instead as plural genitives naming groups of people, which would make Bandua feminine and the partner of a male Bandus, a pair like Bormanos and Bormana or Belisama and Belisamaros. Others have linked the name with water, through the river name Banduje in Portugal, and Krzysztof Tomasz Witczak compares the Illyrian water god Bindus. In 2000 María Prósper derived it from a stem *bandu-, making Bandua a god of passageways like the Roman Ianus. Ladislao Castro Pérez has proposed that St. Torquatus, whose relics are kept at Santa Comba de Bande in Ourense, may be a Christian form of Bandua.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bandua” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They spoke a Celtic language, worshipped gods such as <b>Bandua</b>, and erected stone warrior statues.</blockquote><a href="/state/callaeci">Callaeci</a></li><li><blockquote>They worshipped local gods such as <b>Bandua</b> and Endovelicus, and their warriors fought in light, mobile formations that troubled Roman legions.</blockquote><a href="/state/lusitanians">Lusitanians</a></li></ul></section>
